= Mountbatten and Warrenpoint

On a single August bank holiday, the IRA kills Lord Mountbatten, the Queen's cousin, with a bomb on his fishing boat off the coast of County Sligo. Two teenage boys and an elderly woman die with him. Hours later, two bombs at Warrenpoint, near Newry, kill eighteen soldiers. It is the Army's worst single loss in Northern Ireland.

Two days later you fly to Crossmaglen, in the heart of South Armagh. The right wants a crackdown. The Irish government and the Americans want a political initiative.

- @security: Appoint a security co-ordinator and send more troops.
- @initiative: Launch a political initiative with Dublin.

@security
subtitle: -0.5 budget. The right and the Unionists approve.
on-arrival: budget -= 0.5; right_dissent -= 4; uup_relation += 4; us_relation -= 1

Sir Maurice Oldfield, the former head of MI6, is brought out of retirement to co-ordinate the Army and the police. Another battalion goes to the border.

@initiative
subtitle: Washington and Dublin approve. The Unionists and the right do not.
on-arrival: us_relation += 4; europe_relation += 2; uup_relation -= 6; right_dissent += 4; wet_dissent -= 2

The Northern Ireland Secretary invites the province's parties to a constitutional conference. The Unionists refuse to attend.

