= The Civil Service Strike

The civil service unions have rejected a 7% pay offer. Instead of a general walkout, they are striking at the computer centres and tax offices that keep the money coming in. Billions of pounds of tax are going uncollected, and the court system is grinding to a halt.

- @hold_line: Stand firm on the offer.
- @settle_cs: Settle quickly at a higher figure.

@hold_line
subtitle: -1 budget in lost revenue. Union power falls if we win.
on-arrival: budget -= 1; union_power -= 4; strike_wave += 1; dry_dissent -= 2; wet_dissent += 1

The dispute lasts twenty-one weeks. The unions accept 7.5% in the end, and the Treasury counts the cost of the uncollected tax.

@settle_cs
subtitle: -0.5 budget. The dries call it surrender.
on-arrival: budget -= 0.5; union_power += 2; strike_wave -= 1; dry_dissent += 3

A better offer ends the dispute within the month. Every other public-sector union takes note.

