= Hong Kong

You are in Peking to discuss the future of Hong Kong, whose New Territories return to China when the lease runs out in 1997. Fresh from the Falklands, you want British administration to continue. Deng Xiaoping tells you that China could walk in and take the whole colony that afternoon. Leaving the Great Hall of the People, you stumble on the steps, in front of the cameras.

- @insist: Insist the treaties stand: British administration must continue.
- @flexible: Signal that Britain could accept Chinese sovereignty, with guarantees.

@insist
subtitle: The right approves. Confidence in Hong Kong falls.
on-arrival: hong_kong_firm = 1; right_dissent -= 3; us_relation -= 1

Talks stall. The Hang Seng index falls by a quarter in a month, and the Hong Kong dollar slides.

@flexible
subtitle: The talks can begin. The right calls it another Munich.
on-arrival: right_dissent += 3; us_relation += 1; europe_relation += 1

Formal negotiations begin. The Foreign Office believes a deal guaranteeing Hong Kong's way of life for fifty years is possible.

